mber 3, page 21:"}], "synonyms": ["extend"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To present (something) for sale.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["extend"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(transitive) To present (something) to God or gods, as a gesture of worship or as a sacrifice.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Thou shalt offer every day a bullock for a sin offering for atonement.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Exodus xxix:36:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, of a thing) To present (something) to the sight etc.; to provide for use, consideration etc.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["The city offers beautiful architecture."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["offer up", "showcase"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, engineering) To place (something) in a position where it can be added to an existing mechanical assembly.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The next stage is to remove and replace the top part of the right side lip, and offer the lid to the car to ensure all the shapes and gaps are okay.", "reference": "2009, Roger Williams, Triumph TR2, 3, 3A, 4 & 4A:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To bid, as a price, reward, or wages.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["I offered twenty dollars for it. The company is offering a salary of £30,000 a year."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To happen, to present itself.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The occasion offers, and the youth complies.", "reference": "1697, Virgil, “The Fourth Book of the Georgics”, in John Dryden, transl., The Works of Virgil: Containing His Pastorals, Georgics, and Æneis. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "The opportunity however did not offer till next morning, for Phœbe did not come to bed till long after I was gone to ſleep:", "reference": "1749, [John Cleland], “[Letter the First]”, in Memoirs of a Woman of Pleasure [Fanny Hill], volume I, London: […] [Thomas Parker] for G. Fenton [i.e., Fenton and Ralph Griffiths] […], →OCLC, page 72:"}, {"text": "Much was I disappointed upon learning that the little packet for Nantucket had already sailed, and that no way of reaching that place would offer, till the following Monday.", "reference": "1851 November 14, Herman Melville, chapter 2, in Moby-Dick; or, The Whale, 1st American edition, New York, N.Y.: Harper & Brothers; London: Richard Bentley, →OCLC, page 7:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) To make an attempt; typically used with at.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I will not offer at that I cannot master.", "reference": "a. 1627 (date written), Francis [Bacon], “Considerations Touching a Warre with Spaine. […]”, in William Rawley, editor, Certaine Miscellany Works of the Right Honourable Francis Lo. Verulam, Viscount S. Alban. […], London: […] I. Hauiland for Humphrey Robinson, […], published 1629, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "He would be offering at the shepherd's voice.", "reference": "1692, Roger L’Estrange, “ (please specify the fable number.) (please specify the name of the fable.)”, in Fables, of Æsop and Other Eminent Mythologists: […], London: […] R[ichard] Sare, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "[W]ithout offering at any other Remedy, without taking time to conſider the Conſequences, or to reflect on our own Condition, we haſtily engaged in a War which hath coſt us ſixty Millions; […]", "reference": "1711 December 8 (Gregorian calendar), [Jonathan Swift], The Conduct of the Allies, and of the Late Ministry, in Beginning and Carrying on the Present War, 4th edition, London: […] John Morphew […], published 1711, →OCLC, pages 19–20:"}, {"text": "Here Jones, after expressing the utmost uneasiness, offered to stop her mouth:—“Hey-day! why sure, Mr Jones, you will let me speak; I speaks no scandal, for I only says what I heard from others […]", "reference": "1749, Henry Fielding, The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling, volume (please specify |volume=I to VI), London: A[ndrew] Millar, […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To put in opposition to; to manifest in an offensive or defensive way; to threaten.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["to offer violence to somebody", "The peasants offered no resistance as they were rounded up."], "quotes": [{"text": "A car turned the corner and stopped, and three or four people emerged and approached her, grabbing her purse. When she offered resistance, one of the assailants took out a knife and stabbed her in the lower abdomen and legs.", "reference": "1979 December 15, “Two Assaulted in Bay Village in Separate Incidents”, in Gay Community News, volume 7, number 21, page 3:"}, {"text": "The Viet Cong and NVA tended to offer battle only when they felt that they held a tactical advantage – if they didn't they usually hunkered down in their nearly invisible bunkers and let the Americans pass.", "reference": "2013, Andrew Wiest, Vietnam: A View from the Front Lines, page 125:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɒfə(ɹ)/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɔːfə(ɹ)/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɔfɚ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɑfɚ/", "tags": ["cot caught merger"]}], "forms": [{"word": "offers", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "offre", "tags": ["alternative"]}, {"word": "offa", "tags": ["alternative", "pronunciation-spelling"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(used in combinations from phrasal verbs) agent noun of off", "tags": ["agent", "form of"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Once you finally discover yourself a dismember-er, a de-limber, a fucking head-cutter-offer, the most simple of tasks — enjoying a long walk outside, seeing a movie, conversing with a stranger in the library — all become prized and over-inflated moments of elation.", "reference": "2003, James-Jason Gantt, Losing Summer, →ISBN, page 146:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/offer", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}